1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,334
Two in the morning.

2
00:00:02,334 --> 00:00:04,592
A NICU, somewhere in India.

3
00:00:04,792 --> 00:00:06,590
A baby girl, nine days old.

4
00:00:06,590 --> 00:00:08,029
Born a little early.

5
00:00:08,029 --> 00:00:10,887
Fighting an infection in her blood.

6
00:00:11,087 --> 00:00:13,583
The culture report comes back.

7
00:00:13,583 --> 00:00:16,528
Resistant. Resistant. Resistant.

8
00:00:16,728 --> 00:00:19,987
Outside the glass, her mother has been standing for

9
00:00:19,987 --> 00:00:20,625
hours.

10
00:00:20,825 --> 00:00:22,957
She asks just one question.

11
00:00:22,957 --> 00:00:25,146
Doctor, koi aur dawai hai?

12
00:00:25,896 --> 00:00:27,526
Is there any other medicine?

13
00:00:29,476 --> 00:00:31,813
This story does not begin in the NICU.

14
00:00:31,813 --> 00:00:33,854
It begins in rooms like ours.

15
00:00:34,054 --> 00:00:37,365
A Monday OPD. Forty patients still waiting.

16
00:00:37,365 --> 00:00:38,974
Fever for three days,

17
00:00:38,974 --> 00:00:40,075
runny nose.

18
00:00:40,275 --> 00:00:43,717
Doctor sahab, koi strong antibiotic de dijiye.

19
00:00:43,717 --> 00:00:45,507
Kal office jaana hai.

20
00:00:45,707 --> 00:00:47,096
We know it is viral.

21
00:00:47,096 --> 00:00:50,060
We are tired. And saying no takes time we

22
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:51,143
do not have.

23
00:00:51,343 --> 00:00:55,117
Azithromycin. Three days. Next patient.

24
00:00:55,317 --> 00:00:57,701
Ten seconds. One signature.

25
00:00:57,701 --> 00:00:59,901
It felt like kindness.

26
00:01:00,651 --> 00:01:04,371
Across India, that signature is written again and again.

27
00:01:04,571 --> 00:01:07,145
In a national survey of twenty hospitals,

28
00:01:07,145 --> 00:01:10,651
seven in ten admitted patients were on antibiotics.

29
00:01:10,851 --> 00:01:12,429
More than half of them,

30
00:01:12,429 --> 00:01:13,556
just in case.

31
00:01:13,756 --> 00:01:16,399
Every dose that was not needed is a lesson.

32
00:01:16,399 --> 00:01:18,903
And bacteria never forget a lesson.

33
00:01:19,103 --> 00:01:23,146
In 2017, imipenem, one of our last-line drugs,

34
00:01:23,146 --> 00:01:27,094
still worked on about six in ten Klebsiella samples.

35
00:01:27,294 --> 00:01:30,201
By 2023, barely one in three.

36
00:01:31,151 --> 00:01:33,439
The man with the cold got better in a

37
00:01:33,439 --> 00:01:35,346
week. He always would have.

38
00:01:35,546 --> 00:01:37,656
But the bacteria stayed.

39
00:01:37,656 --> 00:01:39,450
In families. In wards.

40
00:01:39,450 --> 00:01:40,755
In our water.

41
00:01:40,955 --> 00:01:43,560
And they found the ones who could not fight

42
00:01:43,560 --> 00:01:44,108
back.

43
00:01:44,308 --> 00:01:47,080
A newborn. A mother after her C-section.

44
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:49,566
A grandfather on a ventilator.

45
00:01:49,766 --> 00:01:52,273
They never asked for that antibiotic.

46
00:01:52,273 --> 00:01:54,868
They inherited what it left behind.

47
00:01:56,018 --> 00:01:57,056
Back in the NICU,

48
00:01:57,056 --> 00:02:00,021
her mother is still waiting for an answer.

49
00:02:00,221 --> 00:02:03,192
Every prescription we write today is our answer to

50
00:02:03,192 --> 00:02:03,654
her.

51
00:02:04,804 --> 00:02:07,957
Antimicrobial stewardship is not a committee,

52
00:02:07,957 --> 00:02:10,228
or a circular. It is that answer.

53
00:02:10,428 --> 00:02:13,694
Pause, and ask: is this really bacterial?

54
00:02:13,894 --> 00:02:16,361
Send the culture before the first dose.

55
00:02:16,561 --> 00:02:18,104
When the report comes,

56
00:02:18,104 --> 00:02:20,583
narrow the drug, and stop on time.

57
00:02:20,783 --> 00:02:22,680
For surgery, one dose.

58
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:24,158
Not five days.

59
00:02:24,358 --> 00:02:25,871
And teach every patient:

60
00:02:25,871 --> 00:02:28,368
the red line on the strip means only with

61
00:02:28,368 --> 00:02:29,601
a prescription.

62
00:02:30,551 --> 00:02:32,528
AMR Awareness Week has ended.

63
00:02:32,528 --> 00:02:35,331
The bacteria did not take the week off.

64
00:02:35,531 --> 00:02:38,138
This is not one more guideline to forward on

65
00:02:38,138 --> 00:02:39,619
WhatsApp and forget.

66
00:02:39,819 --> 00:02:42,496
It is ten seconds of courage.

67
00:02:42,496 --> 00:02:44,242
To explain. To wait.

68
00:02:44,242 --> 00:02:45,307
To say no.

69
00:02:45,507 --> 00:02:46,621
So that one day,

70
00:02:46,621 --> 00:02:48,014
when a mother asks,

71
00:02:48,014 --> 00:02:49,564
Koi aur dawai hai?

72
00:02:50,514 --> 00:02:52,263
we can still say:

73
00:02:52,263 --> 00:02:53,454
Haan. Hai.
